<article class="env-proposition" id="B5600E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B5600E">Proposition <span data-tag="B5600E">2.3.4.12</span>.</a>hncalC@$\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category and $n \geq 1$. </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
There exists a simplicial set $\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ with the following universal mapping property: $\Fun (K, \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC ) = [K, \calC ]_{n} / \sim $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The simplicial set $\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ is an $n$-category. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
If $\calC $ is an $n$-category, then the natural map $\theta : \calC \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
For every $n$-category $\calD $, composition with $\theta $ induces an isomorphism of simplicial sets 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \psi : \Fun ( \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC , \calD ) \rightarrow \Fun ( \calC , \calD ).  \]
</div>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      To prove $(1)$, we begin by defining $\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC ([m]) = [ \Delta ^ m, \calC ]_{n}/ \sim $, so that the desired universal property holds by definition whenever $K$ is a simplex. Unwinding the definitions, to check the universal property for a general simplicial set $K$ we must verify the following fact: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(\ast )$"><p>
Given two maps $f,g: \bd \Delta ^{n+1} \rightarrow \calC $ which are homotopic relative to $\sk ^{n-1} \Delta ^{n+1}$, if $f$ extends to an $(n+1)$-simplex of $\calC $, then $g$ extends to an $(n+1)$-simplex of $\calC $. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
       This follows easily from Proposition <a href="/tag/F03C0F" data-tag="F03C0F">8.2.3.1</a>. 
    <p>
      We next show that $\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ is an $\infty $-category. Let $\eta _0: \Lambda ^ m_ i \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ be a morphism, where $0 < i < m$. We wish to show that $\eta _0$ extends to an $m$-simplex $\eta : \Delta ^ m \rightarrow \calC $. If $m \leq n+2$, then $\Lambda ^ m_ i = \sk ^{n+1} \Lambda ^ m_ i$, so that $\eta _0$ can be written as a composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Lambda ^ m_ i \rightarrow \calC \stackrel{\theta }{\rightarrow } \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC . \]
</div>
    <p>
       The existence of $\eta $ now follows from our assumption that $\calC $ is an $\infty $-category. If $m > n+2$, then $\Hom _{\sSet }( \Lambda ^ m_ i, \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC ) \simeq \Hom _{\sSet }( \Delta ^ m, \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC )$ by construction, so there is nothing to prove. 
    <p>
      We next prove that $\rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC $ is an $n$-category. It is clear from the construction that for $m > n$, any two $m$-simplices of $\rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ with the same boundary must coincide. Suppose next that we are given two maps $f,f': \Delta ^ n \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC $ which are homotopic relative to $\bd \Delta ^ n$. Let $F: \Delta ^ n \times \Delta ^1 \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ be a homotopy from $f$ to $f'$. Using $(\ast )$, we deduce that $F$ is the image under $\theta $ of a map $\widetilde{F}: \Delta ^ n \times \Delta ^1 \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $, where $\widetilde{F}| \bd \Delta ^ n \times \Delta ^1$ factors through the projection $\bd \Delta ^ n \times \Delta ^1 \rightarrow \bd \Delta ^ n$. Since $n > 0$, we conclude that $\widetilde{F}$ is a homotopy from $\widetilde{F} | \Delta ^ n \times \{ 0\} $ to $\widetilde{F} | \Delta ^ n \times \{ 1\} $, so that $f = f'$. This completes the proof of $(2)$. 
    <p>
      To prove $(3)$, let us suppose that $\calC $ is an $n$-category; we prove by induction on $m$ that the map $\calC \rightarrow \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC $ is bijective on $m$-simplices. For $m < n$, this is clear. When $m = n$ it follows from part $(1)$ of Definition <a href="/tag/FF9854" data-tag="FF9854">2.3.4.1</a>. When $m = n+1$, surjectivity follows from the construction of $h_ n \calC $, and injectivity from part $(2)$ of Definition <a href="/tag/FF9854" data-tag="FF9854">2.3.4.1</a>. For $m > n+1$, we have a commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \Hom _{\sSet }( \Delta ^ m, \calC ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \Hom _{\sSet }( \Delta ^ m, \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC ) \ar [d] \\ \Hom _{\sSet }( \bd \Delta ^ m, \calC ) \ar [r] &amp;  \Hom _{\sSet }( \bd \Delta ^ m, \rm {h}_{n} \!  \calC ) } \]
</div>
    <p>
       where the bottom horizontal map is an isomorphism by the inductive hypothesis, the left vertical map is an isomorphism by construction, and the right vertical map is an isomorphism by Remark <a href="/tag/E8ECFB" data-tag="E8ECFB">2.3.4.4</a>; it follows that the upper horizontal map is an isomorphism as well. 
    <p>
      To prove $(4)$, we observe that if $\calD $ is an $n$-category, then the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Fun ( \calC , \calD ) \rightarrow \Fun ( \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC , \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calD ) \simeq \Fun ( \rm {h}_{n} \!   \calC , \calD ) \]
</div>
    <p>
       is an inverse to $\phi $, where the second isomorphism is given by $(3)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>